/**
 * Wrangler configuration template for dustinedwards.info
 * Copy to wrangler.jsonc and fill in the real resource ids. The real file is
 * gitignored; secrets are set with `wrangler secret put`, never here.
 * https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/wrangler/configuration/
 */
{
	"$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
	"name": "dustinedwards",
	"compatibility_date": "2026-09-01",
	"main": "./workers/app.ts",
	// NO `compatibility_flags`, and the absence is the setting. `nodejs_compat`
	// and `nodejs_compat_v2` are BOTH on by default for a compatibility date of
	// 2026-08-04 or later, so an explicit `nodejs_compat` here would be a flag
	// wrangler ignores and a reader would take for a decision. Measured on the
	// installed runtime before the flag came out: at this date with no flags,
	// `Buffer` is a function and `process` is an object.
	//
	// Turning Node compatibility OFF at this date is `no_nodejs_compat` and
	// `no_nodejs_compat_v2`, which is the pair to reach for if that is ever
	// wanted; removing a positive flag no longer does it.
	"keep_vars": true,
	// THE REAL DOMAIN, as Custom Domains and not route patterns: the zone has no A or CNAME for
	// either name, because a Custom Domain creates its own proxied record. THIS LIST IS THE SOURCE OF
	// TRUTH: `wrangler deploy` overrides routes added in the dashboard with the ones named here, so a
	// domain attached by hand and missing from this list is removed by the next deploy. Deploying this
	// needs Zone > Workers Routes > Write on dustinedwards.info (docs/RUNBOOK.md, CUTOVER.md).
	"routes": [
		{ "pattern": "dustinedwards.info", "custom_domain": true },
		{ "pattern": "www.dustinedwards.info", "custom_domain": true }
	],
	// STATED BECAUSE THE DEFAULT BITES: Wrangler turns the workers.dev address OFF when `routes` is
	// set and this key is absent (measured 2026-10-03: the first deploy with the Custom Domains
	// answered 404 there). The address is still used: the browser check's admin cases reach the
	// layout there, because Access guards /admin on the real domain; the Worker refuses a person
	// there (no Access token is ever minted for it) and sends X-Robots-Tag: noindex.
	"workers_dev": true,
	// INVOCATION LOGS ARE OFF, and that is a PRIVACY setting rather than a cost
	// one. An invocation log is "enriched with information available to
	// Cloudflare in the context of the invocation", which measurably included
	// `request.headers.cookie` and `cf-connecting-ip`: full reader IPs and
	// session cookies were persisted for 7 days at a 100% sampling rate.
	//
	// There is NO field-level redaction. The documented configuration surface is
	// `enabled`, `head_sampling_rate` and `logs.invocation_logs`; nothing masks
	// or excludes an individual request header, so the only mechanism that drops
	// those two fields is dropping the record that carries them.
	//
	// `enabled` STAYS TRUE deliberately. Our own console output and thrown
	// errors are not invocation logs and keep flowing, so visibility into
	// anything the code says about itself is unchanged. What is traded is the
	// automatic per-request record. Visit counts are Cloudflare Web Analytics.
	//
	// TRACES ARE OFF, AND THE `false` IS A RULING RATHER THAN A DEFAULT.
	//
	// Ruled 2026-09-08 by Dustin: export LOGS to Sentry, never traces. The
	// grounds are the same class as the invocation-log decision above, and they
	// are specific rather than general.
	//
	// MEASURED against the current Cloudflare docs 2026-09-07: a fetch handler
	// span carries `url.full`, `url.path` and `url.query` as attributes. This
	// site puts a 43-character CAPABILITY in a PATH at `/preview/<token>`, so
	// enabling traces would ship preview tokens to a third party. That is the
	// exact exposure the retired per-path traffic write was fixed for on
	// 2026-08-15, when the same token was found written verbatim into Analytics
	// Engine, and the fix there was to redact the path before the write.
	//
	// `redact_query_string` DOES NOT HELP, which is the trap worth writing down:
	// the token is in the PATH, not the query string, so the one setting that
	// looks like it addresses this does not.
	//
	// What traces do NOT carry, checked in the same pass so the refusal is not
	// broader than the reason: cookies and client IPs are absent from the
	// documented attribute set. Only the four `url.*` attributes are the problem.
	//
	// STATED EXPLICITLY EVEN THOUGH FALSE IS THE DEFAULT, per the 2026-08-13
	// cold-audit rule in capsid/conventions.md: "an upstream default is not a
	// decision. Where a default is load-bearing for a security or growth
	// property, set it explicitly to the value you want, even when that value is
	// the default." Keep it false in both files, so turning it on is a diff
	// somebody has to argue for.
	//
	// `head_sampling_rate` IS STATED, at 1: every log is kept. Traffic is small and the
	// invocation record that would multiply volume is off, so there is nothing to sample away.
	"observability": {
		"enabled": true,
		"head_sampling_rate": 1,
		"logs": {
			"invocation_logs": false
		},
		"traces": { "enabled": false }
	},
	"upload_source_maps": true,
	// Workers Cache, in front of the Worker. Added for the Images binding: its
	// responses are NOT cached by Cloudflare and every uncached call is a full
	// decode and re-encode, which /media/<key>?w= pays on every request.
	//
	// This is a TOP-LEVEL switch, not an Images setting, so it governs every
	// response the Worker returns. A 200 carrying no Cache-Control is cached
	// for two hours under RFC 9111 heuristic freshness, and cookies are not in
	// the cache key, so an authenticated /admin render would otherwise be
	// stored and served to anyone. `workers/app.ts` closes that by defaulting
	// every response without an explicit Cache-Control to `private, no-store`.
	"cache": { "enabled": true },
	// THE TWO ENTRYPOINTS, and which of them the platform may cache.
	//
	// Ruled 2026-09-05. `workers/app.ts` exports a
	// GATEWAY as `default` and a `Renderer` class. The gateway runs on every
	// request and does only what must not be skipped: the HTTPS redirect, the
	// theme cookie read. It then calls
	// `ctx.exports.Renderer` through a loopback with an explicit `cf.cacheKey`
	// and `{ theme }` in props, and the RENDERER is the entrypoint whose
	// responses are stored.
	//
	// **THE GATEWAY IS CACHE DISABLED, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.** If it were
	// cacheable the platform could answer a request without running it, so the
	// redirect and the cookie read would be skipped on every hit. Disabling it
	// costs one Worker invocation per request. A cache hit still costs no CPU
	// time, per Cloudflare's pricing note.
	//
	// **`cross_version_cache` IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT**, which means it is false
	// and the Worker version stays in the cache key. That is what makes a deploy
	// invalidate every entry, and it is the property that replaced the
	// hand-built `__build` key parameter the old themed layer carried. Turning
	// it on would serve last deploy's HTML, asking for asset URLs the current
	// manifest no longer has.
	//
	// The top-level switch above stays: per-entrypoint settings override it, and
	// leaving it on is what covers the Images binding responses it was added for.
	"exports": {
		"default": {
			"type": "worker",
			"cache": { "enabled": false }
		},
		"Renderer": {
			"type": "worker",
			"cache": { "enabled": true }
		}
	},
	// ONE CRON, AND THIS KEY OWNS THE SET.
	//
	// Every minute `scheduled()` in workers/app.ts runs site-api's runSchedules
	// through site-runtime's runJobs (D1 cron_runs): a live post's scheduled
	// update (the scheduled_updates table) goes live at its time, a post
	// scheduled before it was ever live (visible by its publish_at alone) is
	// announced once it is (scheduled_publishes), and both run the publish hooks:
	// IndexNow, the page purge and webmentions. Failed hooks run again and the
	// trash past 30 days is emptied.
	//
	// The key is never left out. Until 2026-09-07 it was absent, and wrangler then
	// leaves whatever cron is registered on the account alone: an hourly
	// `0 * * * *` created 2026-08-23 fired at a Worker with no `scheduled()` and
	// threw 19 to 44 times a day for fifteen days, unseen, because this file and
	// `wrangler.jsonc` agreed by saying nothing (FAILURES.md, "a construct the scan
	// does not reach is not exempt"). Stated here, the live schedules have
	// something to be compared against in both directions. The same firing also
	// runs the jobs the retired watchdog Worker ran (app/lib/cron-jobs.mjs): the
	// colophon's health snapshot every fifteen minutes, the zero-result purge at
	// 04:00 UTC and the publication check at 06:00 UTC.
	"triggers": { "crons": ["* * * * *"] },
	// THE PUBLICATION CHECK'S MAIL (app/lib/publications/notify.server.ts): a new
	// DOI is mailed once to the secret ALERT_EMAIL, from alerts@ on this zone,
	// which has Email Routing on. No `destination_address`: the generated
	// `SendEmail` type requires `to` anyway, so the secret is the one owner of the
	// address. Carries no account-scoped id.
	"send_email": [{ "name": "EMAIL" }],
	// Static assets, so the Worker can read them through a binding. The Vite
	// plugin supplies `directory` from the client build output; setting it here
	// would fight that.
	"assets": { "binding": "ASSETS" },
	"d1_databases": [
		{
			"binding": "DB",
			"database_name": "dustinedwards",
			"database_id": "00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000",
			"migrations_dir": "drizzle"
		}
	],
	"kv_namespaces": [
		{
			"binding": "APP_KV",
			"id": "00000000000000000000000000000000"
		}
	],
	// Two buckets, split on LIFECYCLE rather than on UI. MEDIA is irreplaceable
	// and is never emptied; OG holds derived cards, which are regenerable by
	// running build:og and so may be emptied and rebuilt by command. Neither
	// carries an account-scoped id, so both lines are complete as written.
	"r2_buckets": [
		{
			"binding": "MEDIA",
			"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-media"
		},
		{
			"binding": "OG",
			"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-og"
		},
		// THE REVISION STORE. Every earlier set of a file's bytes, kept under
		// `replaced/<time>/<key>` before a replace, turn or revert overwrites it
		// (app/lib/media/revisions.server.ts), and read back by a revert. NO SITE
		// CODE PATH EVER DELETES FROM IT: `check:destructive` fails if a delete
		// against this binding appears. It is application data, not a backup: the
		// nightly files backup copies it beside MEDIA into BACKUPS.
		{
			"binding": "MEDIA_REVISIONS",
			"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-media-revisions"
		},
		// THE BACKUPS, nightly (app/lib/backup/d1-backup.server.ts): the D1 dumps under
		// backups/json/, pruned by retention (14 daily, 8 weekly, 6 monthly), and the files
		// backups of MEDIA and MEDIA_REVISIONS under files/media/ and files/revisions/
		// (d1-dump backupFiles). Capsid's off-account copy takes both to Amazon. No preview
		// binding: a preview has nothing to back up.
		{
			"binding": "BACKUPS",
			"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-backups"
		}
	],
	// Media thumbnails. The library derives every size from the one original in
	// R2 through this binding, so without it /media/<key>?w=<n> falls back to
	// serving the full-resolution original and says so in x-media-thumb.
	//
	// Account-scoped rather than zone-scoped, and that is why it is the binding
	// rather than the /cdn-cgi/image/ URL syntax: measured 2026-08-02, the URL
	// interface answers 404 with Cloudflare error 1042 on workers.dev, because
	// it needs a customer zone. Carries no id, so this line is complete as
	// written rather than a placeholder.
	"images": { "binding": "IMAGES" },
	// The CV PDF, rendered in the Worker after each CV save (app/lib/cv/pdf.server.ts) and kept as ONE
	// object in OG. Browser Run through `env.BROWSER.quickAction("pdf", ...)`: no API token and no dashboard
	// opt-in, needs a compatibility date of 2026-03-24 or later (this one qualifies). Free plan: 10 browser
	// minutes a day and 3 concurrent browsers; one render of a few seconds per save is far inside both.
	// Carries no id, so this line is complete as written. Not restated under `previews`: a preview has no CV
	// save, and quickAction has no local emulation.
	"browser": { "binding": "BROWSER" },
	// The deployed version's upload time, which /health's cron check uses to hold back a job that has
	// not had its first window since the deploy (app/lib/health/response.server.ts).
	"version_metadata": { "binding": "CF_VERSION_METADATA" },
	// PREVIEW is empty here and "1" in `previews.vars`, so production never
	// carries it set. workers/app.ts and routes/robots.ts read it for noindex.
	//
	// ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN and ACCESS_AUD name the Cloudflare Access application that guards
	// /admin (dustinedwards-login). Neither is a credential: the AUD tag is public in every
	// token Access issues. app/lib/access.server.ts verifies each request's signed token
	// against them; a Worker without both treats every request as not coming through Access.
	//
	// ACCESS_LAB_AUD names the separate Access application that guards /lab-bench
	// (dustinedwards-lab-login, capsid docs/design/design-identity-roles.md), whose policy allows
	// the lab-members group Capsid keeps equal to /lab-bench/members. Its own tag, so a /admin token is
	// refused at /lab-bench and a /lab-bench token at /admin. Like ACCESS_AUD it is not a credential: the tag is in
	// every token Access issues. Empty, /lab-bench would say it is not set up and admit nobody; it was empty until that
	// application existed.
	//
	// The media suggestions (app/lib/media/suggest.server.ts) call Claude Haiku 5.5 straight at Anthropic with the
	// secret ANTHROPIC_API_KEY (`wrangler secret put`), never through AI Gateway.
	"vars": {
		"PREVIEW": "",
		"ACCESS_TEAM_DOMAIN": "dustinedwards.cloudflareaccess.com",
		"ACCESS_AUD": "8c6b7c7f4c10df60924047ddc1ef3c60054ebbda50609a1d246716f417f63a25",
		"ACCESS_LAB_AUD": "8ec1420e613392850264f5ea03c02479d1fa21c12936625b2c87e62e195d4b80"
	},
	// The site file index write path (site_assets). OG emits a notification, this Worker's queue()
	// handler derives the D1 row. An event notification rather than a dual write:
	// the Worker writes ONLY to R2, so the two systems cannot diverge silently.
	//
	// `max_retries` then the dead-letter queue, because a permanent failure must
	// land somewhere inspectable rather than in a log line nobody reads. The
	// consumer is idempotent by construction (it re-derives from the object as it
	// is now, never from what the message claimed), so a retry is always safe.
	//
	// Queue names carry no account-scoped id, so these lines are complete as
	// written rather than placeholders.
	//
	// Webmention verification (job_a58c8e1be3fc). The route writes the `unverified` row, answers 202 and
	// sends the row id here; queue() fetches the source and records the verdict. A Queue rather than
	// `waitUntil`, because a run cut short in `waitUntil` is lost and this one is retried, then kept in
	// its dead-letter queue. The consumer skips a row already past `unverified`, so a redelivery is a no-op.
	"queues": {
		"producers": [
			{
				"binding": "WEBMENTION_VERIFY",
				"queue": "dustinedwards-webmention-verify"
			}
		],
		"consumers": [
			{
				"queue": "dustinedwards-media-events",
				"max_batch_size": 10,
				"max_batch_timeout": 5,
				"max_retries": 3,
				"dead_letter_queue": "dustinedwards-media-events-dlq"
			},
			{
				"queue": "dustinedwards-webmention-verify",
				"max_batch_size": 10,
				"max_batch_timeout": 5,
				"max_retries": 3,
				"dead_letter_queue": "dustinedwards-webmention-verify-dlq"
			}
		]
	},
	// Ask mode (search Layer 2). Remove this block and Ask disappears cleanly:
	// classic search is D1 only and never waits on it. Instance binding rather
	// than ai_search_namespaces because there is exactly one instance.
	"ai_search": [
		{
			"binding": "AI_SEARCH",
			"instance_name": "dustinedwards"
		}
	],
	// **THIS IS THE WHOLE SITE'S RATE LIMITER. THE NAME SAYS ASK AND THE NAME
	// IS WRONG.** Read this before touching either line below.
	//
	// It began as the two Ask guards, which is where the name comes from: the
	// exact per-IP burst limit (one instance per IP) and the exact daily spend
	// ceiling (one instance, "global"). A Durable Object rather than the
	// `ratelimit` binding or a KV counter because neither of those counts:
	// measured across four runs, the binding refused 1, 2, 9 and then 0 of
	// twelve concurrent requests against a limit of five.
	//
	// EIGHT SUBSYSTEMS NOW METER THROUGH IT, each with its own instance-name
	// prefix: `auth:` (sign-in and /api/auth/*), `op:` (the operator API),
	// `wm:` (webmention), `health:` (/health), `csp:` (/csp-report),
	// plus the smoke actor, preview links and Ask itself. So deleting this
	// block does not "disable Ask": it disables SIGN-IN, and it makes
	// /health answer 503 by design, because a route that cannot reach its
	// limiter must not serve.
	//
	// ## WHY IT IS NOT BEING RENAMED, ruled 2026-09-11
	//
	// The pre-cutover audit raised the name (P2-09) and the honest answer is
	// that renaming it costs far more than the misunderstanding does. Per
	// Cloudflare's own guidance a Durable Object class rename is a
	// THREE-DEPLOY rollout, because the namespace's class pointer and the
	// Worker code that exports the class are not updated atomically. In this
	// repository a deploy is a full deploy.yml run that waits for a green CI
	// run for its exact sha, so that is three deploy windows.
	//
	// And the non-atomic window is the worst one this site has. During it the
	// binding may not resolve, which means sign-in fails, /health answers
	// 503, Capsid's watcher wakes on that 503, and its repair call through the
	// operator API is metered through the same object and cannot get through either. A
	// cosmetic change whose failure mode is a paging storm with the repair
	// door shut is not a change to make in the week before a DNS cutover.
	//
	// There is also no correctness defect to fix. Nothing behaves wrongly; a
	// reader misunderstands. That is what this comment is for.
	//
	// IF IT IS EVER RENAMED, it should move to the declarative `exports` field
	// at the same time, which has replaced the legacy `migrations` array below
	// and expresses a rename as a tombstone rather than an ordered step. That
	// makes it a larger and more deliberate piece of work, not a smaller one.
	// The three-deploy sequence is: alias the new class name to the old in
	// code, deploy; apply the rename while the alias stands, deploy; remove
	// the alias, deploy.
	"durable_objects": {
		"bindings": [
			{
				"name": "ASK_BUDGET",
				"class_name": "AskBudget"
			}
		]
	},
	"migrations": [
		{
			"tag": "v1",
			"new_sqlite_classes": ["AskBudget"]
		}
	],
	// WORKER PREVIEWS (job_c5a6f3e8ce6d). Cloudflare's Git integration builds each
	// PR branch with `npm run build:preview` and publishes it with `npx wrangler
	// preview`; nothing here is read by a production deploy. Previews inherit NO
	// top-level binding, so every binding a page needs is restated below, and each
	// one points at a preview-only resource. Nothing a preview does can reach
	// production data.
	//
	// Deliberately absent: AI_SEARCH (Ask renders off and /search is pure D1), the
	// queue consumers (a Preview cannot consume a Queue, and a Queue has one
	// consumer: https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/previews/resources/#queue-consumers),
	// every secret (so admin and sign-in do not work), and the crons.
	//
	// WEBMENTION_VERIFY is absent too, so /webmention on a preview answers 503 and
	// writes nothing. A producer would have nothing to verify its messages until a
	// Preview can consume, and pointing it at production's queue would put a
	// preview's rows in production's consumer. The Worker tests run the consumer.
	//
	// The two ids are placeholders that scripts/build-preview.mjs fills by looking
	// the resources up BY NAME, so no id is pasted anywhere. The R2 buckets are
	// named, which is all they need. ASK_BUDGET gets a
	// fresh Durable Object namespace per preview automatically; it is restated only
	// because the code reads it from env.
	"previews": {
		"vars": {
			"PREVIEW": "1"
		},
		"d1_databases": [
			{
				"binding": "DB",
				"database_name": "dustinedwards-preview",
				"database_id": "set-by-build-preview",
				"migrations_dir": "drizzle"
			}
		],
		"kv_namespaces": [
			{
				"binding": "APP_KV",
				"id": "set-by-build-preview"
			}
		],
		"r2_buckets": [
			{
				"binding": "MEDIA",
				"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-preview-media"
			},
			{
				"binding": "OG",
				"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-preview-og"
			},
			{
				"binding": "MEDIA_REVISIONS",
				"bucket_name": "dustinedwards-preview-media-revisions"
			}
		],
		"images": { "binding": "IMAGES" },
		"durable_objects": {
			"bindings": [
				{
					"name": "ASK_BUDGET",
					"class_name": "AskBudget"
				}
			]
		}
	}
}
